  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,2),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =2\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot 2}\\,\\text{d}x+0\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot 2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+\u0394x,2+\u0394y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u22482\\,\u0394x+0\\,\u0394y.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n{e}^{(0.3)(2.15)}=f(0.3,2.15),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.3,2.15).$\n\nMoving from $(0,2)$ to $(0.3,2.15),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =0.3-0=0.3, \\\\ \u0394y & =2.15-2=0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u22482\\cdot (0.3)+0\\cdot (0.15) \\\\  & \u22480.6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.6.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.3,2.15) & \u2248f(0,2)+0.6 \\\\ {e}^{(0.3)(2.15)} & \u2248{e}^{(0)(2)}+0.6 \\\\  & \u22481+0.6 \\\\  & \u22481.6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
